! other_cpu_width.strap -- WORD 6 HOLDS THE OTHER-CPU REGISTER IN BITS
! 0-18: a store of ones into word 6 reads back as ones in bits 0-18 and
! zeros in bits 19-63.
!
! THE RULE, read from the page images (2026-11-19):
!   * Reference Manual printed p.35 (PDF p.38), "Other CPU Bits": "In
!     order to permit a program in one computer to signal others, the
!     other CPU register, bits 0-18 of location 6, and the CPU signal
!     indicator, CPUS, have been provided."
!   * pocket card X22-6533-1 (docs/pcard-1.png), SPECIAL STORAGE
!     ASSIGNMENT: "6  Other CPU Bits  19  0-18".
!   * printed p.34 (PDF p.37), "Special Registers": bit positions of
!     locations 0-15 not assigned "are always zero.  Information stored
!     in these positions is not recoverable."
!   So bits 19-63 of word 6 always read zero.  Ones in 0-18 =
!   0xffffe00000000000.
!
! A MODELLING CHOICE THIS DECK PINS.  printed p.36 (PDF p.39): the other
! CPU register "is provided only in computers which are to be used in
! multi-computer systems.  When not present, these bit positions behave
! like the bit positions of address 0" -- that is, a 7030 without the
! feature reads all of word 6 as zero.  The model keeps the register
! PRESENT (bits 0-18 settable; no other computer is signalled), which
! decks/exic_addr.strap also relies on (bit 0 of word 6 as a flag).  If
! the model ever adopts the not-present machine, every pin below
! becomes 0.  Word 6 is not in the protected area (the pocket card
! gives it no P), so no BD is needed; the deck starts with one anyway,
! like ia_width_18bit.
!
! WHY "W6 SYN, 6.0" AND NOT $CPU.  When this deck was written, "$CPU"
! assembled to 11.5, the CPUS bit of the indicator register (first
! draft of this deck, observed), so it spells word 6 by number.  FIXED
! 2026-11-19: "$CPU" is now 6.0, as C22-6531 printed pp.15 and 56 and
! C28-6129 printed pp.10 and 43 print it; the bare branch mnemonic CPU
! (BCPUZ) is still indicator 5 (opcodes.INDICATOR_SYMBOLS).  The deck
! keeps 6.0 so its pins are untouched; tests/test_cpu_symbol.py checks
! that $CPU and 6.0 are the same address.
!
! FOUR STORE PATHS, each read back by an instruction into ordinary
! storage, after Z,W6 clears the word.  SV stores a HALF WORD's bits
! 0-24 (printed p.24), so it is aimed at 6.0 and at 6.32:
!   1. ST(BU,64,8),W6 of 64 ones -- every bit (RB1 at once, RB2 after
!      two NOPs).  Expect 0xffffe00000000000; bit 19 is covered.
!   2. SV,$X1,W6, value all ones -- bits 0-24, so ones land in 19-24
!      (RB3).  Expect 0xffffe00000000000.
!   3. SV,$X1,W6+.32, value all ones -- bits 32-56, wholly unassigned
!      (RB4).  Expect 0.
!   4. SX,$X2,W6 of an index word of all ones -- 64 bits (RB5).
!      Expect 0xffffe00000000000.
!
! RESULT, 2026-11-19: PASS on the fixed model (StretchStorage.bsv's
! addrOtherCpuBits arm keeps {v[63:45], 45'b0}): RB1-RB5 and W6 as
! above, PC 4259.
!
! PROVEN DISCRIMINATING, OBSERVED (a scratch copy of the tree):
!   INJECTION = THE UNFIXED ARM ("addrOtherCpuBits: otherCpuBits <= v;")
!     -> FAIL on 6 pins: RB1 0xffffffffffffffff, RB2 0xffffffffffffffff,
!     RB3 0xffffff8000000000 (bits 0-24), RB4 0x00000000ffffff80 (bits
!     32-56), RB5 0xffffffffffffffff, W6 0xffffffffffffffff (the last
!     store, SX).  PC 4259 passes.
!   The same injection leaves decks/boundary_width.strap passing, and
!   the word-3 injection leaves this deck passing.  Reverted by the
!   inverse substitution; cmp identical to the tracked file.
!
W6      SYN, 6.0                     ! word 6, the other-CPU register
        SLC, (8)4100.0
START   BD,  $+.32
        L(BU,64,8), ONES             ! accumulator := 64 ones
        ST(BU,64,8), W6              ! 1. all of word 6
        L(BU,64,8), W6               !    read back at once
        ST(BU,64,8), RB1
        NOP
        NOP
        L(BU,64,8), W6               !    and again, later
        ST(BU,64,8), RB2
        Z,   W6
        LV,  $X1, ONES               ! value field all ones
        SV,  $X1, W6                 ! 2. STORE VALUE: bits 0-24
        L(BU,64,8), W6
        ST(BU,64,8), RB3
        Z,   W6
        SV,  $X1, W6+.32             ! 3. STORE VALUE at 6.32: bits 32-56
        L(BU,64,8), W6
        ST(BU,64,8), RB4
        Z,   W6
        LX,  $X2, ONES               ! index word all ones
        SX,  $X2, W6                 ! 4. STORE INDEX: all 64 bits
        L(BU,64,8), W6
        ST(BU,64,8), RB5
PASS    BEW, $
        SLC, (8)6000.0
ONES    DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
RB1     DD(BU,64), 0
RB2     DD(BU,64), 0
RB3     DD(BU,64), 0
RB4     DD(BU,64), 0
RB5     DD(BU,64), 0
        END, START
